# Third-person references that are NOT about the owner, declared for
# `noahjefferson:third-person-check.mjs`. Format:
#
#   file | reason | the matched word
#
# A reference to the OWNER is never declarable and never becomes declarable.
# Rewrite it: say the ROLE for a governance fact, and for anything else say what
# was wrong and what it measured.
#
# Checked BOTH WAYS — a declaration matching nothing any more is a failure, so a
# scrub cannot quietly un-cover a file by leaving a stale line here.
#
# `he` IS A RUNWAY END. Runways are identified by their two ends, low and high,
# and every aeronautical dataset this app reads calls them `le` and `he` —
# `he_lat`, `he_lon`, `rw.he`. It is the one pronoun the gate matches on and it
# is also a field name in the schema, so it appears in the data layer, in the
# plan-view renderer that projects it, and in the tests that assert on it.
#
# Renaming the field to dodge the gate would put this repo's navdata out of step
# with the source it parses, which is a worse defect than the false positive.
# Only `he` is declared: a `his` or a `him` in any of these files is still a
# failure, because only `he` is a runway end.
public/src/data/navdata.js | aeronautical schema, the runway's high end | he
public/src/render/gauges/plan.js | aeronautical schema, the runway's high end | he
scripts/airport-picker.test.mjs | aeronautical schema, the runway's high end | he
scripts/build-navdata.test.mjs | CSV double-quote fixture, not a person | He
